B.ce(1, (64, 30, 34),
     'A Spy – Expectation and Reality\n'
     'From films and books, I imagine a secret agent to be tall, handsome, smart and athletic. He wears a dark suit and dark glasses, carries a pistol hidden in his coat and drives a fast car. '
     'He takes part in thrilling chases, fights several enemies at once, climbs buildings, and meets beautiful, mysterious women in dark corners. He speaks little and always looks alert and dangerous.\n'
     'Ausable is just the opposite. He is fat – very fat – and though he speaks French and German passably, he has never lost his American accent. He lives in a small, ordinary room on the sixth floor of a shabby French hotel, and he has no pistol, no car and no fights. '
     'Fowler, a young writer, is disappointed when he sees him.\n'
     'Yet Ausable proves to be a brilliant spy. When Max points a pistol at him, he does not panic. With his presence of mind he invents a story about a balcony and the police, and tricks Max into jumping out of the window. '
     'Ausable shows that a real secret agent needs a sharp mind, calmness and quick thinking more than good looks and weapons.', lid='sr3')
B.ce(1, (64, 35, 39),
     'Horace’s Thoughts on “Honour among Thieves”\n'
     'Sitting in the prison library, Horace Danby often thinks about the lady in red. “I am a thief, but an honest one in my own way,” he thinks. '
     '“I robbed only one safe a year, only to buy rare books, and I never harmed anyone. I believed that even thieves have a code of honour – a thief should never cheat another thief.”\n'
     '“That charming young lady broke that code. She knew I was a burglar, yet she pretended to be the owner’s wife. She played on my good manners and my fear of the police. '
     'Because of her I opened the safe without gloves and left my fingerprints everywhere. She walked away with the jewels, and I was punished for her crime.”\n'
     'Now, whenever anyone talks about “honour among thieves”, Horace feels angry and bitter. He realises that there is no honour among thieves – a person who is dishonest with the world will be dishonest with you too. '
     'His experience also teaches us that crime never pays: the cheater himself gets cheated.', lid='sr4')
